@MaryamAurangze Looking out of my hotel window in Lahore this morning, I see heavy smog once again. Every year brings new announcements and interventions, yet the problem persists. My concern is the quality of technical advice guiding public expenditure. Demonstrated technical competence and accountability must be the basis for selecting advisers and approving solutions.

The Punjab government deserves credit for giving smog greater attention and taking steps to strengthen monitoring, expand cleaner public transport and support crop residue management. These efforts provide a foundation to build on. 

Some interventions, however, warrant closer scrutiny. A published assessment involving EPA Punjab researchers found negligible fine particle recovery by the Mehmood Booti smog tower, with much of the observed improvement attributed to weather. Water cannons may suppress dust locally, but where is the independently verified evidence that they meaningfully reduce Lahore’s exposure to harmful fine particles? Before expanding such interventions, government should publish their results, costs and the technical basis on which they were recommended.

I would urge the Chief Minister Punjab to prioritise four concrete actions:

1. Control emissions from high polluting vehicles through credible testing, fuel quality checks and enforcement, alongside reliable public transport.
2. Verify emissions from industries and brick kilns through actual measurements and sustained inspections. Enforce construction dust controls equally on government and private projects.
3. Give farmers timely, affordable access to crop residue management equipment. Stop open waste burning through reliable collection and address household dependence on polluting fuels.
4. Publish annual pollution reduction targets, assign responsibility to named agencies and independently evaluate results after accounting for weather. Require advisers to demonstrate relevant expertise and subject their recommendations to independent review.

Beijing reduced PM2.5 by 35% between 2013 and 2017 through sustained controls on energy, industry and transport, supported by regional coordination. Punjab should adapt that approach to its own conditions. 
